#X obj 617 8 hw.rpi.gpio.servo;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 486 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 The specified GPIO pin is taken into exclusive ownership:
only one entity can operate a servo motor through this pin.;
#X obj 230 88 cnv 1 2 45 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -221520 -1 0;
#X text 240 138 The servo uses a smoothed trajectory by default \,
ensuring jerk-free motion. This may reduce the top speed marginally.
If you need maximum speed \, disable smoothing by setting @smooth =
0\.;
#X obj 230 143 cnv 1 2 60 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 208 Angle range and pulse width: the servo angle is controlled
by the PWM pulse width. @min_pulse sets the pulse width corresponding
to 0° \, and @max_pulse sets the pulse width corresponding to 180°.
These values are model-specific: for many common servos (e.g. SG90)
the usable range is approximately 0.5–2.5 ms \, while other models
may have a narrower or wider range. Setting the wrong pulse limits
can cause the servo to buzz \, overheat \, or hit a mechanical stop
before reaching the expected angle. Always check the datasheet or calibrate
the limits empirically for your specific servo model.;
#X obj 230 213 cnv 1 2 166 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 384 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 409 • Mechanical movement and robotics — driving a servo
motor from a Pd patch \, for example to open and close a lid \, move
a lever \, or position a small robot arm with smooth \, physically
plausible motion.;
#X text 250 479 • Kinetic sound installations — turning a servo into
part of an art installation \, for example to strike percussion \,
rotate objects or move mirrors and reflectors in time with the music.;
